INT. SELF-SERVE CAR WASH BAY - DAWN

Concrete. Pressure hoses coiled on hooks. Cold sun leaks through the open bay door and lights the soap suds on the floor.

DET. IGNATIUS PELL, 50s, heavy coat, mustard stain on his tie, leans against a wet wall. A cardboard coffee in each hand.

LORETTA "LO" BANKS, 30s, nurse's scrubs under a puffy jacket, hair in a hasty bun, stands by the quarter-slot machine. Her hands are shoved deep in her pockets.

PELL
Thanks for coming.

LO
You said it was about my brother.

PELL
It is.

He holds out a coffee. She doesn't take it.

LO
He's in Ohio.

PELL
He was.

LO
What does that mean?

PELL
Lo.

LO
Don't "Lo" me.

Pell sets the coffee on the machine. Opens his coat. Pulls out a thin folder.

PELL
Found a car three days ago. Under a bridge by Dunmore.

LO
So?

PELL
Plates were Ohio. Registered to Darnell Banks.

Silence. Water drips.

LO
He sold that car.

PELL
Not according to the DMV.

LO
Then someone stole it.

PELL
Someone did. The keys were in the ignition.

She walks to the wall. Presses her forehead against the cold tile.

LO
Was he in it?

PELL
No.

LO
Then what do you want from me?

PELL
His phone pinged a tower four blocks from your apartment. Tuesday night.

She turns, slowly. Her face has changed.

LO
Tuesday.

PELL
Tuesday. Around eleven.

LO
I was on shift.

PELL
Until?

LO
Seven.

PELL
Then who let him in?

LO
Nobody let him in.

PELL
Lo.

She snatches the coffee. Hurls it at the wall. It EXPLODES brown against the tile, hot, steaming.

LO
He's my brother! He's the only family I have, and you come at dawn with a folder and a face like a funeral!

PELL
I'm trying to find him.

LO
You're trying to find me.

He doesn't deny it. He just stands there, coffee dripping down the wall behind her.
